rs and satisfy \\(x+y=c\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\) is a lattice point of the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).  \n   Moreover, for a fixed diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) the \\(k\\) points \\((i(c-i),c-i^{2})\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) are pairwise distinct, because the first coordinate equals \\(i(c-i)\\) and the function \\(i\\mapsto i(c-i)\\) is injective on \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\). Hence each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) contributes exactly one point to each sunny line \\(L_{i}\\).  \n\n   *Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together contain **all** points of the \\(k\\) shortest diagonals.  \n\n   *Finally, for the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonals \\(D_{c}\\;(c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) we simply take the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=c\\qquad(c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1),\n   \\]\n\n   which are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are not sunny.  \n\n   *Altogether we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines; every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); the necessary condition shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible.}}\n\\]"
