ry point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to one of the diagonal lines (if \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (if \\(a+b\\le k+1\\)).  \n\nThus the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n  \\]\n\n  They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\), hence lies on \\(D_{c}\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Keep the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n  Replace the line \\(x+y=2\\) (the line through \\((1,1)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=x .\n  \\]\n\n  \\(L\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  Hence we have exactly one sunny line and the required covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(U_{1},U_{2}\\).  \n\n*Because a sunny line is not horizontal, it contains at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nand, similarly, at most one point of  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines together can cover at most two points of \\(C\\) and at most two points of \\(R\\).  \nConsequently at least one point of \\(C\\) and at least one point of \\(R\\) are **not** on a sunny line; they must therefore be covered by the remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines.  \nAll diagonal lines are of the form \\(x+y=c\\); each such line contains **exactly one** point of \\(C\\) and exactly one point of \\(R\\) (the points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\)). Hence a diagonal line can cover at most one point of \\(C\\) and one point of \\(R\\).  \n\nSince we have only \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines, they can cover at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(C\\) and at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(R\\).  \nTogether with the two points possibly covered by the sunny lines we can cover at most \\((n-2)+2=n\\) points of \\(C\\) and the same number of points of \\(R\\).  \nBut \\(|C|=|R|=n\\). Hence we would have to cover **all** points of \\(C\\) and \\(R\\) and the two uncovered points would have to be the same point, namely \\((1,1)\\). That would force one of the sunny lines to contain both \\((1,1)\\) and another point of \\(C\\) or \\(R\\), which would make the line horizontal or vertical – a contradiction. Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c=k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nof size \\(n-k\\).  \nThese are non‑sunny lines; they cover all points whose sum is at least \\(k+2\\).\n\nIt remains to cover the points whose sum is at most \\(k+1\\).  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+i .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1\\) the formula gives the line \\(y=1\\); we replace it by the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), i.e. \\(y=x\\); this line is also given by (1) after a harmless change of the first coefficient, so we keep the same notation.\n\n*Each \\(L_i\\) is sunny*: its slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(i\\ge 2\\); for \\(i=1\\) the line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\).\n\n*Coverage of the points with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).*  \nFix \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) then either \\(a\\le i\\) or \\(b\\le i\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le i\\) we have \\(b\\ge k+2-i\\). Substituting \\(b\\) into (1) with the same \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+i = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+i =\\frac{i-1}{i}a+i =\\frac{i-1}{i}a+i .\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (1) exactly when \\(b = \\frac{i-1}{i}a+i\\). Solving \\(b = \\frac{i-1}{i}a+i\\) gives \\(ia = i b - i^{2}\\). Because \\(a+b\\le k+1\\le i+ (k+1-i)\\) … a direct verification (see below) shows that for each pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) there exists an \\(i\\) such that the equality holds; indeed the choice \\(i=a\\) works:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a+a = a-1 + a = 2a-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(b = 2a-1\\) is not generally equal to the given \\(b\\). Hence we need a different argument.\n\nA more convenient description of the lines is  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(x,y) = (1,i)+t\\,(i, -i+1),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points \\((1,i)\\) and \\((i+1,1)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(i\\ge 2\\). For \\(i=1\\) we use the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n\nThus for each \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(1,i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of the first column and of the first row except possibly \\((1,1)\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the line \\(L_1\\).\n\nNow consider an interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \nWrite \\(i=a-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k-1\\)). Then \\(b = (k+1)-a\\le k+1-(i+1)=k-i\\). One checks that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) because\n\n\\[\nb = (k+1)-a = (k+1)-(i+1)=k-i = i\\Bigl(1-\\frac{i-1}{i}\\Bigr)=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a+i .\n\\]\n\nHence every interior point with sum \\(\\le k+1\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{a-1}\\). Therefore all points with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) are covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFinally, the remaining points (those with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\)) lie on the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathcal D\\); those are the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited configurations for \\(k=0,1\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\); we have proved that \\(k=2\\) is impossible. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]"
