ber of omitted diagonals equals \\(n-d\\).  Condition (2) therefore says\nthat the set of omitted diagonals must contain at least \\(c-1\\) members.\n\n*If \\(c\\ge 4\\).*  \nThen \\(c-1\\ge 3\\); we would have to omit at least three diagonals.\nThe three smallest possible omitted diagonals are\n\\(D_{2},D_{3},D_{4}\\); together they contain  \n\n\\[\n|D_{2}|+|D_{3}|+|D_{4}|=1+2+3=6\n\\]\n\npoints.  Since a line can meet a given diagonal in at most one point,\ncovering those six points would require at least six distinct lines,\nwhereas we would have only \\(n-d\\le 3\\) lines left – a contradiction.\nThus no diagonal with \\(c\\ge 4\\) can be omitted.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\).*  \nThen (2) forces \\(n-d\\ge2\\); i.e. at least two diagonals must be omitted.\nThe only way to omit two diagonals is to omit \\(D_{2}\\) and \\(D_{3}\\).\nTheir total number of points is \\(1+2=3\\), but three points lying on two\ndifferent diagonals need at least three distinct lines (a line can contain at\nmost one point of \\(D_{3}\\)).  Yet we would have only \\(n-d=2\\) lines left,\nagain impossible.  Hence \\(D_{3}\\) cannot be omitted either.\n\nConsequently the only diagonal that may be omitted is \\(D_{2}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Consequences of Lemma 1\n\nBecause all diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\) must be present, any admissible\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines consists of\n\n* the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_{3},D_{4},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), and\n* one additional line.\n\nIf the additional line is non‑sunny, it has to be parallel to one of the three\nforbidden directions.  The only diagonal not already present is \\(D_{2}\\);\nhence the extra line can be \\(D_{2}\\) itself.  This yields a configuration\nwith **no** sunny line.\n\nIf the additional line is sunny, it cannot be parallel to any of the three\ndirections.  A sunny line can contain at most one point of each diagonal,\nso it can meet the diagonal \\(D_{2}\\) only at its unique point \\((1,1)\\).\nTherefore the extra sunny line must pass through \\((1,1)\\); any such line is\nsunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Taking, for instance,\nthe line \\(y=x\\) gives a configuration with **exactly one** sunny line.\n\nThus the only possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines\nexists.  By Lemma 1 all diagonals \\(D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) must be among the\nchosen lines; consequently the only diagonal that could be omitted is \\(D_{2}\\).\nBut at most **one** sunny line can replace \\(D_{2}\\) (it must pass through the\nsingle point \\((1,1)\\)).  Hence at most one sunny line can appear, contradicting\n\\(k\\ge2\\).  Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit constructions\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{c}=D_{c}\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n  \\]\n\n  They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of\n  \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Start from the previous family and replace the line\n  \\(L_{2}=D_{2}\\) (which contains only \\((1,1)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  S:\\;y=x .\n  \\]\n\n  The point \\((1,1)\\) remains covered, all other points are unchanged, and\n  exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\nBoth constructions use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and the arguments above\nshow that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]"
