            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction for the attainable values**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – keep the same family and replace the line \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only the point \\((1,1)\\)) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)**  \n   *Key observation.*  Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the unique diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{c}\\;:\\;x+y=c,\\qquad c=a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A **sunny** line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it meets each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in **exactly one** point (or none).  \n   Consequently a sunny line can cover at most one point of each diagonal.\n\n   *Counting argument.*  \n   For a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) let \\(|D_{c}|\\) denote the number of lattice points it contains; one easily checks  \n\n   \\[\n   |D_{c}|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   If a diagonal is **not** used as one of the \\(n\\) lines, every point of it that is not already covered by a sunny line must be covered by a non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or another diagonal).  \n   Any non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal itself meets \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point, so to cover the remaining \\(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\) points (where \\(s_{c}\\) is the number of sunny lines intersecting \\(D_{c}\\)) we would need at least \\(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   Hence, for each diagonal we have the following optimal choice:\n\n   * either we **include** the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) as a line (cost = 1 line, covering all its points),  \n   * or we **do not** include it and then we must spend at least \\(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   Summing over all diagonals gives a lower bound for the total number of lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\n   \\underbrace{\\#\\{c:\\,D_{c}\\text{ is used}\\}}_{\\text{diagonals taken}}+\n   \\sum_{c\\notin T}\\bigl(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\ge\\; n,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(T\\) is the set of indices of the diagonals that are taken.\n\n   *Application to the problem.*  \n   For \\(c\\ge 4\\) we have \\(|D_{c}|\\ge 3\\).  \n   Since each sunny line meets a diagonal in at most one point, we have \\(s_{c}\\le k\\).  \n   If \\(k\\ge 2\\) then for every \\(c\\ge 4\\)\n\n   \\[\n   |D_{c}|-s_{c}\\;\\ge\\;3-2\\;=\\;1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, **if we do not take a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge 4\\) we would need at least one extra non‑sunny line for that diagonal**.  \n   To keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) we are forced to take **all** diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such diagonals, so (1) becomes\n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-2)\\;\\ge\\; n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at most two sunny lines can appear.\n\n   *Excluding the case \\(k=2\\).*  \n   When \\(k=2\\) the two sunny lines can each cover at most one point of the diagonals \\(D_{2}\\) (the single point \\((1,1)\\)) and \\(D_{3}\\) (the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \n   Because a line containing \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\), it is **not sunny**.  \n   Moreover a sunny line cannot contain \\((1,1)\\) together with either \\((1,2)\\) (vertical) or \\((2,1)\\) (horizontal).  \n   Consequently the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) require **three** distinct sunny lines, contradicting \\(k=2\\).\n\n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible, and by (2) no larger \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n   Combining the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) with the impossibility proof for \\(k\\ge 2\\) yields the final answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal\n\n\\[\nD_{c}:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on exactly one diagonal \\(D_{c}\\); conversely  \n\n\\[\n|D_{c}|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(D_{2}\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}=D_{c}\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nThey are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to \\(D_{2}\\), therefore none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nStart from the previous family and replace the line \\(L_{2}=D_{2}\\) (which contains only \\((1,1)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) remains covered, all other points are unchanged, and exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nFor each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ns_{c}=|\\{\\,\\text{sunny lines intersecting }D_{c}\\,\\}|\\; .\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line meets a diagonal in at most one point,  \n\n\\[\n0\\le s_{c}\\le k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}s_{c}=k\\,(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) is **not** among the \\(n\\) chosen lines, then every point of \\(D_{c}\\) not already covered by a sunny line must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \nA non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal itself meets \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point; consequently we need at least \\(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\) such lines.\n\nLet \\(T\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be the set of indices of the diagonals that are taken as lines.  \nCounting all lines gives\n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;|T|\\;+\\;\\sum_{c\\notin T}\\bigl(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\bigr)\\;\\ge\\;n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) and (4) we rewrite (5) as\n\n\\[\nk+|T|+\\Bigl(\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|D_{c}|\\Bigr)-\\sum_{c\\in T}|D_{c}|-\n\\Bigl(k(n-1)-\\sum_{c\\in T}s_{c}\\Bigr)\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|D_{c}|=|\\mathcal P|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), after simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\nk+|T|-\\sum_{c\\in T}|D_{c}|+\\sum_{c\\in T}s_{c}\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor every diagonal with index \\(c\\ge 4\\) we have \\(|D_{c}|\\ge 3\\) and, by (4), \\(s_{c}\\le k\\).  \nThus for such a diagonal  \n\n\\[\n-|D_{c}|+s_{c}\\;\\le\\;-3+k .\n\\]\n\nIf we omitted any diagonal with \\(c\\ge 4\\) (i.e. \\(c\\notin T\\)), the left‑hand side of (5) would increase by at least \\(1\\) (the term \\(|D_{c}|-s_{c}\\ge 1\\)).  \nTo keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) we must therefore **include all diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\)**.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |T|=n-2 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7) into (5) gives\n\n\\[\nk+(n-2)\\;\\ge\\;n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2 . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus at most two sunny lines can appear in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Excluding the case \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nBy (7) the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines must be the diagonals \\(D_{4},D_{5},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently the only points not already lying on a diagonal are those of \\(D_{2}\\) and \\(D_{3}\\):\n\n\\[\nD_{2}=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad D_{3}=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two sunny lines must cover these three points.  \nA line containing both \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **not sunny**.  \nA sunny line cannot contain \\((1,1)\\) together with \\((1,2)\\) (that would be vertical) nor with \\((2,1)\\) (that would be horizontal).  \nHence each of the three points requires a distinct sunny line, contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines are present.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=2\\) does not exist.  \nCombined with (8) we conclude that **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe attainable values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).  \nBoth are realizable by the explicit constructions given in Section 1, and any larger \\(k\\) is impossible by the counting argument of Sections 2–3.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
